  "note": "After replacing in_cycle (constant-zero and useless - the transaction graph is a DAG, so cycles cannot exist) with fan_ratio (a DAG-appropriate fan-in/fan-out balance measure) and fixing a label-leakage bug in community_illicit_ratio (see README, Methodology fix), Baseline has the best F1 (0.80) of the XGBoost variants. Graph-augmented and Hybrid both push precision above 0.98 but recall falls to ~0.6-0.64, netting a lower F1 than the baseline. AUC-PR - which does not depend on a classification threshold - tells a more balanced story: baseline (0.800) and graph-augmented (0.794) are close, meaning the graph-augmented model ranks transactions by risk about as well as the baseline overall, but its default 0.5 probability threshold produces a specific high-precision/low-recall operating point rather than a genuinely weaker model. GCN remains the weakest model on every metric (F1 0.45, AUC-PR 0.45), consistent with the original Elliptic benchmark paper (Weber et al., 2019), where a similarly simple GCN was beaten by a Random Forest with hand-engineered features. The Hybrid model (F1 0.79, AUC-PR 0.79) shows the same precision/recall tradeoff as graph-augmented - concatenating the GCN's embeddings on top of the hand-engineered features didn't add clean predictive signal here."
}